{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012TbbzedgqH8SpPkErmoaP6","type":"tool_result","content":"## Reviewer loop\n\nReviewers run like workers against a PR instead of an issue: they read the change, never edit code, and return a verdict the controller turns into PR comments and a label.\n\n1. **Poll and claim** open PRs with `ard:review:<role>`, skipping claimed, done, changes, failed and held. Reviewers get first call on free job slots, since each finished review can unblock a merge. Only PRs the App opened are reviewed: the author is the App, the base is `main` and the head is a `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` branch (human PRs are skipped and logged as `not_app_pr`). A PR is also skipped when its issue is closed, held or `ard:needs-human`, when a worker job for the issue is running, when the PR has `ard:tests:failed`, and when the issue's title or body changed after admission (the issue is re-gated exactly as the worker does, not reviewed). Reviewers of different roles run in parallel on one PR.\n2. **Pin the head SHA.** Before claiming, the loop fetches the branch into the controller's clone and requires the tip to equal the PR's head SHA; on a mismatch it retries on the next poll. The match also shows the head is this repository's branch, so a fork PR with a colliding branch name is never reviewed. The job's worktree is cut at that commit, so the review describes exactly one commit even if the branch moves.\n3. **Run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hide a change, with this issue's `.ard/<issue#>/` left out and cut at 1 MiB), `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, never committed), the committed `.ard/<issue#>/` (plan, triage and worker role files and logs) and the sidecar (security policy, license allowlist). The loop runs every git command against the controller clone, never in the reviewer's worktree (the agent itself may run read-only git there). In a later round it also gets `.ard-input/<issue#>/previous-review.md`, its own earlier report, read from the pinned head, an\n4. **The agent writes `review-<role>.md`** with `verdict: approve | changes` and `findings:` (`file`, `line`, `severity`, `blocking`, `message`; see the .ard/ contract). It may write nothing else under `.ard/<issue#>/`, and code edits are never collected. An `approve` that carries a blocking finding is treated as `changes` (the job succeeds and the comment says so).\n5. **Close-out.** The controller posts one PR review at the pinned commit (event `COMMENT`): findings on lines the PR added are inline comments, every other finding is listed in the review body. If GitHub answers 422 (a line or the commit is not in the PR), it posts the review again with every finding in the body, and if that is also refused, as a plain PR comment. It commits `review-<role>.md` and the run's redacted output to the PR branch through the per-branch committer (no `[skip ci]`: PR-branch commits always run CI so required checks report on the head), and swaps `:running` for `:done` or `:changes`. The reviewer never edits the PR. After a close-out (and on every tick, so a restart cannot miss it) the controller marks a draft worker PR ready for review once every requested review is `:done` at the PR's current head and no `:changes`, `:running`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-\n6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the request label stays and the review runs again on the new head. The reviewed commit is recorded in the close-out record so the merger (#46) re-verifies, with `prgate.Covers` against the reviewed head recorded by the review's claim, that no code of the PR changed since before merging: a small window remains between the head check and close-out adding `:done`. A review closed out by the Reaper after a restart has no head information, so an approve is treated as stale.\n7. **A gone PR is stale, not failed.** A human may merge or close the PR, or GitHub may delete its branch, while a review runs. At close-out the controller reads the PR before committing. If it was merged or closed, or the committer reports the branch gone, the job ends `stale`: nothing is committed to the branch, only `:running` is removed, there is no `ard:executor:failed` and no failure comment, and `review.pr_gone` is logged with the state (`merged`, `closed` or `branch_gone`). The PR review is still posted, best-effort: if posting fails the error is logged and the job still closes. A failed PR read falls through to the commit, where a missing branch gives the same outcome. Gate 2's failure rate counts only `failed` jobs, so these are not executor failures. A worker has no equivalent: its PR is a draft that cannot be merged while it runs, so a branch deleted mid-job stays a real fail\n- #63 Limits-aware scheduling: stop starting agent jobs as the Claude subscription nears its 5-hour or weekly limit\n- #64 Token caps per role, alongside #57's dollar caps\n- #183 Version every PR merge to main as vX.Y.Z\n- #205 Reviewer aid step: run pinned deterministic tools before a reviewer agent\n- #224 Scanner: trufflehog history scan\n- #239 Limits-aware scheduling: stop starting agent jobs as the Claude subscription nears its 5-hour or weekly limit, part B: thresholds and the one-job rule\n├── triage/<name>/       # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── workers/<name>/      # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── reviewers/<name>/    # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n└── <issue#>/            # per-issue output and logs, written only by the committer\n```","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53a4b6cd-1947-4c9b-876e-e420c4c51cec","uuid":"66b0bf88-1c8e-4c5f-aee4-b32256854472","timestamp":"2026-11-24T21:46:39.445Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Reviewer loop\n\nReviewers run like workers against a PR instead of an issue: they read the change, never edit code, and return a verdict the controller turns into PR comments and a label.\n\n1. **Poll and claim** open PRs with `ard:review:<role>`, skipping claimed, done, changes, failed and held. Reviewers get first call on free job slots, since each finished review can unblock a merge. Only PRs the App opened are reviewed: the author is the App, the base is `main` and the head is a `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` branch (human PRs are skipped and logged as `not_app_pr`). A PR is also skipped when its issue is closed, held or `ard:needs-human`, when a worker job for the issue is running, when the PR has `ard:tests:failed`, and when the issue's title or body changed after admission (the issue is re-gated exactly as the worker does, not reviewed). Reviewers of different roles run in parallel on one PR.\n2. **Pin the head SHA.** Before claiming, the loop fetches the branch into the controller's clone and requires the tip to equal the PR's head SHA; on a mismatch it retries on the next poll. The match also shows the head is this repository's branch, so a fork PR with a colliding branch name is never reviewed. The job's worktree is cut at that commit, so the review describes exactly one commit even if the branch moves.\n3. **Run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hi